<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.6.3: Connecting Graphical and Verbal Representations of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00945</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.3: Connecting Graphical and Verbal Representations of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7585884a-37f5-4d07-b422-092ff567a6fd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity </title>
      <para>In a bungee jump, the height of the jumper is a function of time since the jump begins.</para>
      <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
        <!-- Graph Cell -->
        <div class="graph-cell">
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Graph of curve. Horizontal axis, 0 to 35 by 5’s, t, seconds. Vertical axis, 0 to 90 by 10’s, h, meters. Curve starts a 0 comma 80, decreases then increases, 4 times.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_06_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        
        <!-- Expressions and Equations Cell -->
        <div class="expressions-cell">
          <para>Expressions and Equations:</para>
          <list>
            <item>\(h(0)\)</item>
            <item>\(h(t)=0\)</item>
            <item>\(h(4)\)</item>
            <item>\(h(t)=80\)</item>
            <item>\(h(t)=45\)</item>
          </list>
        
        <!-- Features Cell -->
        <div class="features-cell">
          <para>Features:</para>
          <list>
            <item>first dip in the graph</item>
            <item>vertical intercept</item>
            <item>first peak in the graph</item>
            <item>horizontal intercept</item>
            <item>maximum</item>
          </list>
        
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Match each description about the jump to a corresponding expression or equation and to a feature on the graph.</item>
      </list>
      <para>One expression or equation does not have a matching verbal description. Its corresponding graphical feature is also not shown on the graph. Interpret that expression or equation in terms of the jump and in terms of the graph of the function. Record your interpretation in the last row of the table.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <para>Description of Jump</para>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <para>Expression or Equation</para>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
                <para>Feature of Graph</para>
             </emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                <item>the greatest height that the jumper is from the river</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                <item>the height from which the jumper was jumping</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                <item>the time at which the jumper reached the highest point after the first bounce</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                <item>the lowest point that the jumper reached in the entire jump</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
                <item>the time at which the jumper hits the surface of the river</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(t)=80\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>maximum</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(0)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>vertical intercept</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(t)=45\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>first peak in the graph</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(4)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>first dip in the graph; also the minimum of the graph shown</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Description of Jump</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>the time at which the jumper hits the surface of the river</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(t)=0\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>horizontal intercept</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>2. Use the graph to:</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>estimate \(h(0)\) and \(h(4)\).</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answers:</para>
          <para>\(h(0)\) is 80, and \(h(4)\) is about 10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- Question 2 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If \(t=4\), then \(h(4)\) equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(h(4) \approx 10\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 3 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If \(h(t) = 45\), then \(t\) equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The approximate solutions to \(h(t) = 45\) are \(t = 2\) and \(t = 9\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 4 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If \(h(t) = 0\), then \(t\) equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>There is no solution to \(h(t) = 0\) when \(t\) is between 0 and 35, which is what is shown on the graph. We don't know if the function will have a solution when \(t\) is greater than 35.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: The Meaning of Function Notation</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about connecting graphical and verbal representations of a function.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/08336f60e67c7d416294bbeff0c6da750df4db0f"/>
      </media>
      <para>The horizontal intercept is also called the \(x\)-<term class="glossary-tooltip">intercept,</term> or <term class="glossary-tooltip">zero</term>.</para>
      <para>The vertical intercept is also called the \(y\)<term class="glossary-tooltip">-intercept</term>.</para>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Based on the information available, how long do you think the bungee cord is? Make an estimate and explain your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answers:</para>
          <para>About 50 meters. The low points on the graph increase with each bounce, and the high points decrease. They look like they are approaching about 30 meters in height above the river. Since the jump started from 80 meters above the river, this means that the bungee cord is about 50 meters long.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <title>Self Check</title>
      <para>The graph models the height, in feet, of a kickball over time. When is the ball at its maximum height?</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS THE HEIGHT OF A KICKBALL IN FEET AS A FUNCTION OF TIME. THE FUNCTION IS MODELED BY AN UPSIDE DOWN PARABOLA WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 0, \(x\)-intercepts OF 0 AND 5, AND MAXIMUM AT THE POINT (2.5, 31).  ">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>More Real-Life Graphs</title>
      <para>The graph below represents the total number of smartphones that are shipped to a retail store over the course of 50 days.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS THE NUMBER OF SMARTPHONES SHIPPED TO A RETAIL STORE AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN DAYS. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 50 IN INTERVALS OF 10. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 4000 IN INCREMENTS OF 1000. THE GRAPH IS INCREASING LINEARLY FROM X = 0 TO X = 20, REMAINS CONSTANT FROM X = 20 TO X = 22, AND INCREASES FROM X = 22 TO X = 50.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>Why does \(f(20) = f(22)\)?</para>
      <para>Since section B is not changing, it represents the period of time where the store did not receive new shipments, so \(f(20)=f(22)\).</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: More Real-Life Graphs</title>
      <para>The relationship between Jameson’s account balance \(B\), in dollars, and time \(t\), in days, is modeled by the graph below.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS ACCOUNT BALANCE IN DOLLARS AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN DAYS. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 14 IN INTERVALS OF 2. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 100 IN INTERVALS OF 20. THE GRAPH IS CONSTANT FROM X = 0 TO X = 6, INCREASES FROM X = 6 TO X = 9, REACHING A MAXIMUM AT THE POINT (9, 100), AND THEN DECREASES.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>What does the point \(B(9)=98\) represent in the context of the problem?</para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to describe what \(B(9)=98\) represents in the context of the problem:</para>
            <para>\(B(9)=98\) represents the maximum amount of money Jameson had in his account over the time period shown. It also represents that after 9 days, Jameson had $98 in his account. It is the maximum because it is the highest point on the graph provided.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
    </section>
    
